      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mountain Road Historic District earned spots on the Virginia Landmarks Register and National Register of Historic Places in 1983.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/230-0078/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Named after White Oak Mountain, Mountain Road stretches west from the courthouse square into Halifax County’s rolling countryside.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/230-0078/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Residences along Mountain Road showcase restrained late Federal, Greek Revival, Italianate, Queen Anne, and Colonial Revival styles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/230-0078/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early houses were designed by Dabney Cosby Jr., son of Jeffersonian craftsman Dabney Cosby Sr.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/230-0078/",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A 2015 boundary increase added one mile west along US 360, extending the district’s period of significance to 1965.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/230-0078/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Most historic homes are private, but many are visible from the public right‑of‑way; drivers should respect owner privacy.",
